    ans: "Cancer is a disease in which abnormal cells divide uncontrollably because normal regulation of cell growth and division has been disrupted. It commonly develops through the accumulation of mutations in genes that control the cell cycle. Some cancers form tumours, although cancers such as leukaemia may not form solid tumours.",
    why: "Genes that regulate cell division normally control when cells grow, divide or undergo programmed cell death. Mutations can disrupt these controls, allowing abnormal cells to multiply and sometimes invade surrounding tissues or spread to other parts of the body.",
