    ans: "The orange-red precipitate indicates the presence of reducing sugars, such as glucose. Benedict's solution changes from blue through green, yellow and orange to brick-red depending on the amount of reducing sugar present. Heating in a hot water bath allows the reaction to occur.",
    why: "Reducing sugars donate electrons to copper(II) ions in Benedict's solution under alkaline conditions, reducing them to copper(I) oxide, which forms a coloured precipitate. The final colour gives an approximate indication of reducing sugar concentration, but the test is not specific to glucose alone. A negative result generally remains blue."
