  {
    q: "Organisms living in different environments show features that help them survive. A polar bear has thick fur, a cactus stores water, and a fish has a streamlined body. Identify ten examples of adaptations and explain how each helps an organism survive.",
    hint: "Think about structural, behavioural and physiological features that improve survival in particular environments.",
    steps: [
      "Identify the environmental challenge faced by each organism.",
      "Identify the feature or behaviour that helps overcome that challenge.",
      "Explain how the feature improves survival or reproductive success.",
      "Remember that adaptations must be inherited characteristics of populations, not temporary changes acquired by individuals."
    ],
    ans: [
      "Thick fur in polar bears provides insulation and reduces heat loss in cold environments.",
      "Camouflage coloration helps organisms avoid predators or approach prey without being detected.",
      "Webbed feet in ducks increase the surface area for pushing against water during swimming.",
      "The long neck of a giraffe helps it reach leaves high in trees and can also assist in competition for food.",
      "Sharp claws in predators help पकड़ and hold prey or defend against threats.",
      "Nocturnal behaviour allows some desert animals to avoid extreme daytime heat and reduce water loss.",
      "Water-storing tissues in cacti help them survive long periods with little rainfall.",
      "Seasonal migration allows animals to move to areas with more favourable food, breeding or climatic conditions.",
      "Deep roots in some plants help access water from deeper soil layers.",
      "A streamlined body in fish reduces resistance as they move through water."
    ],
    why: "Adaptations are inherited structural, physiological or behavioural characteristics that improve an organism's ability to survive and reproduce in a particular environment. Natural selection can increase the frequency of advantageous inherited characteristics over generations. A feature is not necessarily an adaptation simply because it is useful in one situation."
  }
