add(
  "biology",
  "nervous_system",
  "sense_organs_specialised_receptors",
  "The Eye (Structure, Function & Seeing Process)",

  `<h2>The Eye (Structure, Function & Seeing Process)</h2>
<p>The eye is a <b>sensory organ for vision</b>. It detects light and converts it into electrical signals that the brain interprets as images.</p>

<hr>

<h3> 1. MAIN PARTS OF THE EYE</h3>

<div style="text-align:center; margin: 14px 0;">
  <img src="/images/human_eye.png" alt="Anatomy of the Human Eye" style="width:100%; max-width:340px; max-height:220px; object-fit:contain; border-radius:10px; box-shadow: 0 3px 12px rgba(0,0,0,0.45); display:block; margin:0 auto;" />
  <p style="font-size:0.8em; color:#a0a0b0; margin-top:6px;"><b>Fig:</b> Human Eye Cross-Section</p>
</div>

<ul>
  <li><b>Cornea:</b> Transparent outer layer that protects the eye and helps focus light</li>
  <li><b>Iris:</b> Colored part that controls the size of the pupil</li>
  <li><b>Pupil:</b> Opening that allows light into the eye</li>
  <li><b>Lens:</b> Focuses light onto the retina</li>
  <li><b>Retina:</b> Light-sensitive layer where image is formed</li>
  <li><b>Optic nerve:</b> Carries visual signals to the brain</li>
  <li><b>Aqueous humor:</b> Fluid that maintains eye pressure</li>
  <li><b>Vitreous humor:</b> Jelly-like substance that maintains eye shape</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> 2. HOW THE EYE SEES (STEP-BY-STEP PROCESS)</h3>

<p>The process of seeing happens in a precise sequence:</p>

<pre>
Light → Cornea → Pupil → Lens → Retina → Optic nerve → Brain
</pre>

<h4> Step 1: Light enters the eye</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Light from an object enters through the cornea</li>
</ul>

<h4> Step 2: Pupil controls light entry</h4>
<ul>
  <li>The iris adjusts the pupil size depending on light intensity</li>
  <li>Bright light → pupil becomes small</li>
  <li>Dim light → pupil becomes large</li>
</ul>

<h4> Step 3: Light is focused by the lens</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Lens changes shape to focus light</li>
  <li>Ensures image is sharp</li>
</ul>

<h4> Step 4: Image forms on retina</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Retina receives a real, inverted (upside-down) image</li>
  <li>Light is converted into electrical signals</li>
</ul>

<h4> Step 5: Signals go to brain</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Optic nerve carries signals to the brain</li>
  <li>Brain interprets the image correctly (upright)</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> 3. HOW WE ACTUALLY “SEE”</h3>

<ul>
  <li>The eye does NOT see directly</li>
  <li>The retina only detects light</li>
  <li>The brain interprets signals as images</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Key idea:</b> Seeing happens in the brain, not just the eye.</p>

<hr>

<h3> 4. FUNCTION OF EACH PART</h3>

<ul>
  <li><b>Cornea:</b> Protects and bends light</li>
  <li><b>Iris:</b> Controls light entering the eye</li>
  <li><b>Pupil:</b> Entry point for light</li>
  <li><b>Lens:</b> Focuses image</li>
  <li><b>Retina:</b> Detects light and forms image</li>
  <li><b>Optic nerve:</b> Sends signals to brain</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> 5. COMMON EYE ADAPTATION</h3>

<ul>
  <li><b>Dark adaptation:</b> Eye becomes sensitive in dark conditions</li>
  <li><b>Light adaptation:</b> Eye adjusts in bright light</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> 6. COMMON EYE DEFECTS (BASIC)</h3>

<ul>
  <li><b>Short-sightedness (myopia):</b> Cannot see far objects clearly</li>
  <li><b>Long-sightedness (hypermetropia):</b> Cannot see near objects clearly</li>
  <li><b>Cataracts:</b> Lens becomes cloudy</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> 7. REAL LIFE EXAMPLE OF SEEING</h3>

<p><b>Seeing a book:</b></p>

<ul>
  <li>Light reflects from the book</li>
  <li>Enters eye through cornea</li>
  <li>Pupil adjusts light amount</li>
  <li>Lens focuses image on retina</li>
  <li>Optic nerve sends signal to brain</li>
  <li>Brain recognizes it as a book</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> SUMMARY</h3>

<pre>
Eye = light detector
Retina = image formation + signal conversion
Optic nerve = signal carrier
Brain = image interpreter
Seeing = brain process, not just eye
</pre>
`,
[
  {
    q: "A learner looks at a moving football and is able to identify its position, shape and movement. Which organ detects the light coming from the football and makes vision possible?",
    hint: "Consider the sensory organ that contains a light-sensitive layer.",
    steps: [
      "Identify what must enter a sensory organ for an object to become visible.",
      "Determine which organ detects light from objects.",
      "Explain how this organ begins the process of vision."
    ],
    ans: "The eye detects light from objects and converts it into electrical signals that are transmitted to the brain for visual processing.",
    why: "The eye collects and focuses light onto the retina. Photoreceptor cells in the retina respond to light and generate signals that travel through the optic nerve to the brain, enabling vision."
  },

  {
    q: "A person has a healthy cornea and lens, but damage to the light-sensitive layer at the back of the eye causes severe vision loss. Which structure is damaged, and what is its function?",
    hint: "Think of the structure containing the rods and cones.",
    steps: [
      "Identify where light must be focused inside the eye.",
      "Determine which cells detect light intensity and colour.",
      "Explain how these cells contribute to visual information."
    ],
    ans: "The retina is damaged. It contains photoreceptor cells called rods and cones that detect light and convert it into electrical signals.",
    why: "The retina lines the back of the eye and contains rods, which are important for vision in dim light, and cones, which support colour vision and detailed vision in brighter light. These photoreceptors initiate signals that are processed by retinal nerve cells and transmitted towards the brain."
  },

  {
    q: "A patient can detect light with the retina, but damage to the nerve connecting the eye to the brain prevents normal transmission of visual information. Which structure is damaged?",
    hint: "Consider the pathway that carries visual signals away from the retina.",
    steps: [
      "Identify where light is converted into electrical signals.",
      "Determine which structure carries visual information away from the eye.",
      "Explain why damage to this structure affects vision."
    ],
    ans: "The optic nerve.",
    why: "The optic nerve carries electrical signals generated by retinal cells towards the brain. The brain processes these signals to produce visual perception. Damage to the optic nerve can interrupt this pathway and cause vision loss."
  },

  {
    q: "A student observes that the image formed on a screen by a convex lens is upside down. The same basic effect occurs when light is focused by the eye onto the retina. Why is the image formed on the retina inverted?",
    hint: "Consider how light rays from the top and bottom of an object behave when they pass through a convex lens.",
    steps: [
      "Trace the path of light rays entering the eye from the top of an object.",
      "Compare their path with rays coming from the bottom of the object.",
      "Explain what happens when the rays are refracted and cross before reaching the retina."
    ],
    ans: "The image is inverted because the cornea and convex lens refract light rays so that rays from the top and bottom of the object cross before reaching the retina.",
    why: "The eye's optical system bends incoming light and focuses it onto the retina. Light rays from the top of an object are directed towards the lower part of the retina, while rays from the bottom reach the upper part. The resulting retinal image is therefore inverted. The brain processes this visual information to produce our normal perception of the object's orientation."
  },

  {
    q: "A person walks from a brightly lit outdoor area into a dark room. Their pupils gradually become larger. Which structure controls this change, and why does the pupil enlarge?",
    hint: "Think about the coloured part of the eye containing muscles around the pupil.",
    steps: [
      "Identify the opening through which light enters the eye.",
      "Determine which structure contains muscles that change the size of this opening.",
      "Explain why allowing more light into the eye is useful in darkness."
    ],
    ans: "The iris controls pupil size. In dim light, its radial muscles contract and its circular muscles relax, causing the pupil to enlarge and allow more light into the eye.",
    why: "The iris regulates the amount of light entering the eye by changing pupil diameter. In bright light, circular muscles contract and radial muscles relax, making the pupil smaller. In dim light, radial muscles contract and circular muscles relax, making it larger. This adjustment helps control the amount of light reaching the retina."
  },

  {
    q: "A person has healthy eyes and functioning retinal photoreceptors, but damage to the brain's visual processing areas prevents them from consciously recognising objects. What does this demonstrate about where seeing actually occurs?",
    hint: "Distinguish between detecting light and interpreting the information carried by visual signals.",
    steps: [
      "Identify the role of photoreceptors in the retina.",
      "Determine where signals from the retina are processed.",
      "Explain why light detection alone is not sufficient for conscious vision."
    ],
    ans: "Conscious visual perception occurs in the brain. The eyes detect light and convert it into electrical signals, while the brain processes these signals to recognise and interpret what is being seen.",
    why: "The retina converts light into nerve signals that travel through the optic nerve and visual pathways to the brain. The visual cortex and other brain regions process information such as shape, colour, position and movement. Damage to these processing areas can prevent normal visual recognition even when the eyes detect light."
  }
]);
