add(
  "biology",
  "nervous_system",
  "sense_organs_specialised_receptors",
  "The Ear (Structure, Function & Hearing Process)",

  `<h2>The Ear (Structure, Function & Hearing Process)</h2>
<p>The ear is a <b>sensory organ for hearing and balance</b>. It detects sound vibrations and converts them into electrical signals that the brain interprets as sound.</p>

<hr>

<h3> 1. MAIN PARTS OF THE EAR</h3>

<div style="text-align:center; margin: 14px 0;">
  <img src="/images/human_ear.png" alt="Anatomy of the Human Ear" style="width:100%; max-width:340px; max-height:220px; object-fit:contain; border-radius:10px; box-shadow: 0 3px 12px rgba(0,0,0,0.45); display:block; margin:0 auto;" />
  <p style="font-size:0.8em; color:#a0a0b0; margin-top:6px;"><b>Fig:</b> Human Ear – Outer, Middle & Inner</p>
</div>

<h4> A. Outer Ear</h4>
<ul>
  <li><b>Pinna (auricle):</b> Collects sound waves</li>
  <li><b>Ear canal:</b> Carries sound to eardrum</li>
</ul>

<h4> B. Middle Ear</h4>
<ul>
  <li><b>Eardrum (tympanic membrane):</b> Vibrates when sound hits it</li>
  <li><b>Ossicles (tiny bones):</b> Hammer, anvil, stirrup — amplify sound</li>
</ul>

<h4> C. Inner Ear</h4>
<ul>
  <li><b>Cochlea:</b> Converts vibrations into nerve signals</li>
  <li><b>Auditory nerve:</b> Sends signals to the brain</li>
  <li><b>Semicircular canals:</b> Help with balance</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> 2. HOW HEARING WORKS (STEP-BY-STEP PROCESS)</h3>

<pre>
Sound waves → Outer ear → Middle ear → Inner ear → Auditory nerve → Brain
</pre>

<h4> Step 1: Sound is collected</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Pinna collects sound waves from the environment</li>
</ul>

<h4> Step 2: Sound travels through ear canal</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Sound waves move into the ear canal</li>
</ul>

<h4> Step 3: Eardrum vibrates</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Sound waves hit the eardrum</li>
  <li>Eardrum vibrates according to sound intensity</li>
</ul>

<h4> Step 4: Vibrations are amplified</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Ossicles (hammer, anvil, stirrup) amplify vibrations</li>
</ul>

<h4> Step 5: Signals reach cochlea</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Cochlea converts vibrations into electrical signals</li>
</ul>

<h4> Step 6: Signals go to brain</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Auditory nerve carries signals to brain</li>
  <li>Brain interprets them as sound</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> 3. HOW WE ACTUALLY HEAR</h3>

<ul>
  <li>The ear detects sound vibrations only</li>
  <li>The cochlea converts vibrations into nerve impulses</li>
  <li>The brain interprets impulses as meaningful sounds</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Key idea:</b> Hearing happens in the brain, not just the ear.</p>

<hr>

<h3> 4. FUNCTION OF EACH PART</h3>

<ul>
  <li><b>Pinna:</b> Collects sound</li>
  <li><b>Ear canal:</b> Transmits sound inward</li>
  <li><b>Eardrum:</b> Vibrates with sound</li>
  <li><b>Ossicles:</b> Amplify vibrations</li>
  <li><b>Cochlea:</b> Converts sound into electrical signals</li>
  <li><b>Auditory nerve:</b> Sends signals to brain</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> 5. EAR AND BALANCE</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Semicircular canals detect head movement</li>
  <li>Help maintain body balance</li>
  <li>Send signals to brain about position</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> 6. REAL LIFE EXAMPLE OF HEARING</h3>

<p><b>Hearing music:</b></p>

<ul>
  <li>Sound waves travel through air</li>
  <li>Pinna collects the sound</li>
  <li>Ear canal directs sound to eardrum</li>
  <li>Eardrum vibrates</li>
  <li>Ossicles amplify vibrations</li>
  <li>Cochlea converts them into signals</li>
  <li>Brain interprets sound as music</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> 7. COMMON EAR PROBLEMS</h3>

<ul>
  <li><b>Ear infection:</b> Pain and swelling in ear</li>
  <li><b>Hearing loss:</b> Damage to eardrum or cochlea</li>
  <li><b>Blocked ear canal:</b> Wax buildup reduces hearing</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> SUMMARY</h3>

<pre>
Ear = sound detector
Cochlea = signal converter
Auditory nerve = signal carrier
Brain = sound interpreter
Hearing = brain process using ear signals
</pre>
`,
[
  {
    q: "A learner hears a school bell ringing and turns towards the sound. Which organ detects the sound and also contains structures responsible for maintaining balance?",
    hint: "Think of the sensory organ responsible for both hearing and equilibrium.",
    steps: [
      "Identify the type of stimulus produced by the ringing bell.",
      "Determine which sensory organ detects sound vibrations.",
      "Recall which sensory organ also contains structures that help maintain balance."
    ],
    ans: "The ear detects sound and contains structures responsible for maintaining balance.",
    why: "The ear has specialised structures for two functions. The cochlea detects sound, while the vestibular system, including the semicircular canals and vestibule, detects head movement and position to help maintain balance."
  },

  {
    q: "A person can hear sound vibrations reaching the inner ear, but damage to the cochlea prevents these vibrations from being converted into normal nerve signals. Which function of the cochlea has been disrupted?",
    hint: "Consider what must happen to mechanical vibrations before the brain can interpret sound.",
    steps: [
      "Identify the type of energy carried by sound vibrations.",
      "Determine what sensory cells inside the cochlea detect.",
      "Explain how mechanical vibrations are converted into electrical signals."
    ],
    ans: "The cochlea converts mechanical sound vibrations into electrical nerve signals through specialised sensory hair cells.",
    why: "Sound vibrations create movement in the fluid inside the cochlea. This movement causes structures within the cochlea to stimulate sensory hair cells. The hair cells convert the mechanical stimulation into electrical signals that activate auditory nerve fibres."
  },

  {
    q: "A person suffers damage to the eardrum and consequently has difficulty hearing sounds clearly. Explain what normally happens to the eardrum when sound waves enter the ear.",
    hint: "Consider the first structure in the middle-ear transmission pathway that moves in response to sound.",
    steps: [
      "Identify what travels through the ear canal.",
      "Determine which structure is struck by the incoming sound waves.",
      "Explain how its movement transfers vibrations to the next structures."
    ],
    ans: "The eardrum vibrates when sound waves strike it and transfers these vibrations to the ossicles in the middle ear.",
    why: "Sound waves travel through the ear canal and cause the thin eardrum to vibrate. These vibrations are passed to the three middle-ear bones, allowing sound energy to be transmitted towards the inner ear."
  },

  {
    q: "A patient has damage to the three tiny bones in the middle ear. Sound reaches the eardrum, which vibrates normally, but the vibrations are not transmitted effectively to the inner ear. Which structures are damaged, and what is their normal function?",
    hint: "Think about the three connected bones that transfer vibrations from the eardrum to the oval window.",
    steps: [
      "Identify the bones located immediately behind the eardrum.",
      "Recall their sequence from the eardrum towards the inner ear.",
      "Explain how they help transmit and increase the effectiveness of sound vibrations."
    ],
    ans: "The ossicles: the malleus (hammer), incus (anvil) and stapes (stirrup). They transmit and amplify vibrations from the eardrum to the oval window of the inner ear.",
    why: "The ossicles form a mechanical chain that transfers vibrations from the eardrum to the inner ear. Their lever action and the difference in surface area between the eardrum and oval window increase pressure at the oval window, helping sound vibrations move the cochlear fluid."
  },

  {
    q: "A person has a functioning eardrum, ossicles and cochlea, but damage to the nerve pathway carrying information from the cochlea prevents normal hearing. Which nerve pathway is affected, and what does it normally do?",
    hint: "Consider the sensory pathway that connects the inner ear with the brain.",
    steps: [
      "Identify where sound vibrations are converted into electrical signals.",
      "Determine which pathway carries these signals away from the cochlea.",
      "Explain what happens when this pathway is damaged."
    ],
    ans: "The auditory branch of the vestibulocochlear nerve (cranial nerve VIII) carries nerve impulses from the cochlea towards the brain.",
    why: "Sensory hair cells in the cochlea stimulate auditory nerve fibres. These fibres transmit electrical signals through the auditory pathway to the brain, where sound information is processed. Damage to this pathway can cause hearing impairment."
  },

  {
    q: "A person has functioning ears and can detect sound vibrations, but damage to the brain's auditory processing regions prevents them from correctly recognising spoken words. What does this reveal about where hearing perception occurs?",
    hint: "Distinguish between detecting sound in the ear and interpreting its meaning.",
    steps: [
      "Identify what the ear does when sound waves enter it.",
      "Determine where electrical signals from the cochlea are processed.",
      "Explain why detecting sound does not automatically mean understanding it."
    ],
    ans: "Conscious hearing and interpretation occur in the brain. The ear detects sound and converts vibrations into nerve signals, while the brain processes these signals to recognise sounds and interpret speech.",
    why: "The cochlea converts mechanical vibrations into electrical signals, which travel along the auditory pathway to the brain. Auditory regions of the brain process characteristics such as pitch, loudness and speech patterns. Understanding spoken language also involves other specialised brain regions."
  }
]);
