add(
  "biology",
  "nervous_system",
  "sense_organs_specialised_receptors",
  "The Skin (Structure, Function & Touch Process)",

  `<h2>The Skin (Structure, Function & Touch Process)</h2>
<p>The skin is the <b>largest sensory organ</b> in the human body. It detects touch, pressure, temperature, and pain, and sends signals to the brain for interpretation.</p>

<hr>

<h3> 1. MAIN LAYERS OF THE SKIN</h3>

<div style="text-align:center; margin: 14px 0;">
  <img src="/images/skin.png" alt="Anatomy of Human Skin" style="width:100%; max-width:340px; max-height:220px; object-fit:contain; border-radius:10px; box-shadow: 0 3px 12px rgba(0,0,0,0.45); display:block; margin:0 auto;" />
  <p style="font-size:0.8em; color:#a0a0b0; margin-top:6px;"><b>Fig:</b> Skin Layers & Sensory Receptors</p>
</div>

<h4> A. Epidermis</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Outermost protective layer</li>
  <li>Acts as a barrier against germs and injury</li>
  <li>Contains no blood vessels</li>
</ul>

<h4> B. Dermis</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Middle layer of the skin</li>
  <li>Contains nerve endings, blood vessels, sweat glands</li>
  <li>Main sensory layer for touch</li>
</ul>

<h4> C. Subcutaneous Layer (Fat Layer)</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Inner layer made of fat</li>
  <li>Insulates body and absorbs shock</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> 2. SENSORY RECEPTORS IN THE SKIN</h3>

<ul>
  <li><b>Touch receptors:</b> Detect light contact</li>
  <li><b>Pressure receptors:</b> Detect strong force</li>
  <li><b>Pain receptors:</b> Detect injury or harm</li>
  <li><b>Temperature receptors:</b> Detect heat and cold</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> 3. HOW TOUCH WORKS (STEP-BY-STEP PROCESS)</h3>

<pre>
Stimulus → Skin receptors → Sensory nerve → Spinal cord → Brain → Sensation (touch/pain/temperature)
</pre>

<h4> Step 1: Stimulus occurs</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Something touches the skin (heat, cold, pressure, pain)</li>
</ul>

<h4> Step 2: Receptors detect stimulus</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Nerve endings in the skin detect changes</li>
</ul>

<h4> Step 3: Signal formation</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Receptors convert stimulus into electrical nerve impulses</li>
</ul>

<h4> Step 4: Signal travels</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Sensory nerves carry impulses to the spinal cord</li>
</ul>

<h4> Step 5: Brain receives signal</h4>
<ul>
  <li>Brain processes and interprets the information</li>
</ul>

<h4> Step 6: Sensation is felt</h4>
<ul>
  <li>You feel touch, pain, heat, or cold</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> 4. HOW WE ACTUALLY FEEL TOUCH</h3>

<ul>
  <li>The skin only detects physical changes</li>
  <li>Nerves carry signals to the brain</li>
  <li>The brain interprets signals as sensation</li>
</ul>

<p><b>Key idea:</b> Feeling happens in the brain, not just the skin.</p>

<hr>

<h3> 5. TYPES OF SENSATIONS</h3>

<ul>
  <li><b>Touch:</b> Light contact (e.g., clothing on skin)</li>
  <li><b>Pressure:</b> Strong force (e.g., carrying heavy bag)</li>
  <li><b>Pain:</b> Injury or damage (e.g., cut or burn)</li>
  <li><b>Temperature:</b> Heat or cold changes</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> 6. FUNCTION OF EACH SKIN PART</h3>

<ul>
  <li><b>Epidermis:</b> Protection against injury and infection</li>
  <li><b>Dermis:</b> Contains receptors and nerves for sensation</li>
  <li><b>Fat layer:</b> Insulation and shock absorption</li>
  <li><b>Nerve endings:</b> Detect stimuli</li>
  <li><b>Sensory nerves:</b> Carry signals to brain</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> 7. REAL LIFE EXAMPLE OF TOUCH</h3>

<p><b>Touching a hot object:</b></p>

<ul>
  <li>Skin detects heat</li>
  <li>Thermoreceptors send signal</li>
  <li>Sensory nerves carry impulse to spinal cord</li>
  <li>Brain receives and interprets pain</li>
  <li>Body reacts (withdraws hand)</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> 8. IMPORTANCE OF SKIN AS A SENSE ORGAN</h3>

<ul>
  <li>Protects body from injury and germs</li>
  <li>Helps detect dangerous conditions</li>
  <li>Regulates body temperature</li>
  <li>Enables awareness of environment</li>
</ul>

<hr>

<h3> SUMMARY</h3>

<pre>
Skin = largest sense organ
Receptors = detect touch, pain, temperature
Nerves = carry signals
Brain = interprets sensation
Feeling = brain-based interpretation of skin signals
</pre>
`,
[
  {
    q: "A learner accidentally touches a hot cooking pot and immediately feels heat and pain. Which organ detects these changes and helps protect the body from injury?",
    hint: "Think about the organ that covers the body and contains specialised sensory receptors.",
    steps: [
      "Identify the organ that comes into direct contact with the hot surface.",
      "Determine which structures detect changes such as heat and pain.",
      "Connect stimulus detection with the body's awareness of potential danger."
    ],
    ans: "The skin detects heat and pain through specialised sensory receptors and helps protect the body by alerting it to potentially harmful stimuli.",
    why: "The skin contains sensory receptors that detect changes in the environment, including touch, pressure, temperature and pain. It also forms a protective barrier against physical injury, pathogens and water loss."
  },

  {
    q: "A patient suffers damage to the dermis of the skin and subsequently loses some ability to detect touch, pressure and temperature in the affected area. Which structures may have been damaged?",
    hint: "Look for specialised structures within the skin that detect changes in the environment.",
    steps: [
      "Identify the skin layer affected by the injury.",
      "Recall which structures detect different types of stimuli.",
      "Explain how damage to these structures affects sensation."
    ],
    ans: "Sensory receptors in the dermis may have been damaged.",
    why: "The dermis contains many sensory nerve endings and specialised receptors that detect touch, pressure, temperature and pain. Damage to these receptors or their nerve connections can reduce sensation. Some sensory nerve endings also extend into other skin layers."
  },

  {
    q: "A person touches a sharp object. Receptors in the skin detect the stimulus, but the person cannot consciously feel the pain because the sensory pathway to the central nervous system is damaged. What role would the damaged sensory nerves normally perform?",
    hint: "Consider what happens to a message after a receptor detects a stimulus.",
    steps: [
      "Identify where the stimulus is first detected.",
      "Determine how information travels from the skin towards the central nervous system.",
      "Explain what happens when this communication pathway is interrupted."
    ],
    ans: "Sensory nerves carry nerve impulses from receptors in the skin towards the spinal cord and brain.",
    why: "Sensory receptors detect stimuli and generate electrical signals. Sensory neurons transmit these impulses towards the central nervous system for processing. Damage to the sensory pathway can prevent or reduce the transmission of information, resulting in loss of sensation."
  },

  {
    q: "A person touches a rough surface. Receptors in the skin detect the contact, and nerve impulses travel towards the central nervous system. Where is the information processed to produce the conscious sensation of touch?",
    hint: "Distinguish between the organ that detects a stimulus and the organ that interprets it.",
    steps: [
      "Identify where the physical stimulus is detected.",
      "Determine which part of the nervous system receives sensory information.",
      "Identify where the information is interpreted as a conscious sensation."
    ],
    ans: "The brain processes the sensory information and produces the conscious sensation of touch.",
    why: "The skin detects the stimulus through sensory receptors, but it does not independently interpret the sensation. Nerve impulses travel through sensory pathways to the brain, where sensory information is processed and interpreted."
  },

  {
    q: "A learner is examining four different objects: a soft cotton ball, a heavy book pressing against the hand, a hot cup, and a sharp pin. Which four types of skin receptors are needed to detect the different stimuli?",
    hint: "Classify each stimulus according to the type of information it provides to the nervous system.",
    steps: [
      "Identify the stimulus produced by the cotton ball.",
      "Distinguish the sustained force of the book from ordinary light contact.",
      "Identify the receptors needed to detect heat and potentially damaging stimulation."
    ],
    ans: "Touch receptors, pressure receptors, temperature receptors and pain receptors (nociceptors).",
    why: "Different sensory receptors respond to different types of stimuli. Touch receptors detect contact, pressure receptors respond to mechanical pressure, temperature receptors detect changes in temperature, and nociceptors detect potentially harmful stimuli that can cause tissue damage."
  },

  {
    q: "A person accidentally steps on a sharp object while walking barefoot. Explain how the skin helps the person detect the danger and why this ability is important for survival.",
    hint: "Think about the relationship between detecting harmful stimuli, sending nerve impulses and responding to danger.",
    steps: [
      "Identify the receptors that detect potentially harmful stimulation.",
      "Trace how the resulting nerve impulses travel towards the central nervous system.",
      "Explain how awareness of pain helps the person respond to the danger."
    ],
    ans: "The skin contains pain receptors that detect potentially harmful stimuli. Sensory nerves transmit impulses towards the central nervous system, allowing the person to become aware of the injury and respond by moving away from the harmful object.",
    why: "Pain sensation acts as a protective warning system. It alerts the nervous system to possible tissue damage and encourages behaviours that reduce further injury. The skin also protects the body physically against pathogens, injury and excessive water loss."
  }
]);
